  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Primarily dry weather is expected through this \nweekend, but periodic rain showers are possible this Thursday \nevening through Thursday night and again this Sunday. Near-record\nwarmth is forecast this Thursday.\n\nA surface ridge continues to affect our region today through\ntonight as the embedded high pressure center moves SE'ward from\nnorthern ON toward northern New England, while the ridge aloft \nbuilds from the Upper Midwest to the eastern Great Lakes. Dry \nweather is expected as stabilizing subsidence accompanies the \nridge. Widespread low-level clouds, which appear to be \nassociated with moist isentropic ascent ahead of a 925-700 mb \nshortwave trough axis oriented generally from west to east and \nembedded in NW'erly mean flow in that atmospheric layer, should \nexit our CWA generally from north to south by daybreak this \nmorning. Sufficient cloud breaks and weak or calm surface winds \nshould contribute to localized steam fog in the Black River \nValley and the inland lake and river valleys of the Western \nSouthern Tier and Finger Lakes Region during the predawn hours \nthrough about 10 AM EDT today. However, a sufficiently-cold air\nmass, NE'erly to ENE'erly mean flow in the surface to 925 mb \nlayer, and weak/shallow lake-induced CAPE profiles amidst \nlimited synoptic-scale low-level moisture over ~14C Lake \nAmerica should allow isolated to scattered and shallow lake-\neffect stratocumuli to impact portions of Western and Central \nNY, especially metro Rochester and the northern Finger Lakes \nRegion, through mid-morning. Any lake-effect clouds should \ndissipate by midday as lake-induced CAPE dissipates via low-\nlevel dry air advection and a lowering subsidence inversion.\n \nLows are still expected to reach mainly the 20's to mid 30's \naround daybreak this morning and be followed by highs in the mid \n40's to lower 60's late this afternoon as low-level CAA persists \nbehind yesterday's cold front passage. Weak or calm surface winds, \nlow humidity, and sufficient cloud breaks, especially farther east, \nare expected to permit appreciable radiational cooling very late \nthis afternoon through daybreak Wednesday. Lows should reach mainly \nthe lower 20's to lower 30's in North-Central NY and the lower 30's \nto lower 40's elsewhere in our CWA around daybreak Wednesday.\n\nOn Wednesday through Wednesday night, the ridge at the surface \nand aloft should exit E'ward and allow a warm front starved of \nlow-level moisture to sweep NE'ward through our region and \nusher-in a somewhat warmer air mass. Fair weather is expected to\npersist. Late afternoon highs should reach the 50's to lower \n60's in North-Central NY and the upper 50's to upper 60's \nelsewhere in our CWA. Thereafter, overnight lows should reach \nthe mid 30's to mid 40's in North-Central NY and the lower 40's\nto lower 50's elsewhere in our CWA around daybreak Thursday. \n\nMainly W'erly to WNW'erly flow aloft and a series of attendant \ntroughs and ridges at the surface and aloft should affect our \nregion this Thursday through upcoming weekend. Current odds \nfavor primarily dry weather as ridging should affect Western and\nCentral NY during the majority of the time period. However, a \nweak cold front should sweep E'ward through our region Thursday \nnight. Periodic rain showers should impact Western and Central \nNY Thursday evening and especially Thursday night, along and \nahead of the front, due to moist isentropic ascent ahead of a \nshortwave trough axis aloft and low-level convergence/ascent \nalong the front coinciding with sufficient low-level moisture. \nAdditional periodic rain showers are possible this Sunday due to\nmoist isentropic ascent preceding another shortwave trough axis\naloft. Unusually-mild weather for late October is expected on \nThursday through this weekend courtesy of largely zonal flow \naloft. For example, near-record warm highs in the upper 60's to\nmid 70's are expected in many lower elevation locations in our \nWestern NY and Finger Lakes counties this Thursday afternoon, \nincluding the Buffalo and Rochester metro areas.",
